<details>
	<summary>What do the three test categories check?</summary>
	<div>
		<ul class="keep-ls">
			<li><b>Cosmetic Filter</b>: whether empty ad boxes, cookie banners and similar elements are hidden or removed.</li>
			<li><b>Script Loading</b>: whether ad scripts and trackers are stopped from loading.</li>
			<li><b>Host</b>: whether domains that serve trackers, analytics or ads are blocked.</li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</details>
<details>
	<summary>What do &ldquo;Blocked&rdquo; and &ldquo;Not Blocked&rdquo; mean?</summary>
	<div>
		<div class="row keep-width">
			<div>
				<div class="card _h-100">
					<div class="_f-center">
						<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" stroke-width="2" stroke="var(--green)" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 0h24v24H0z" stroke="none"></path><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"></circle><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
						&nbsp; <b>Blocked</b>
					</div>
					The connection to a host or script failed, or an ad box was removed, so your ad blocker caught it.
				</div>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="card _h-100">
					<div class="_f-center">
						<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" stroke-width="2" stroke="var(--red)" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 0h24v24H0z" stroke="none"></path><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"></circle><path d="M10 10l4 4m0-4l-4 4"></path></svg>
						&nbsp; <b>Not Blocked</b>
					</div>
					The connection went through, or the box stayed in place, so that item was not filtered by your setup.
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</details>
<details>
	<summary>How is my score calculated, and what do the colors mean?</summary>
	<div>
		<p>When the tests finish you get a liquid-filled score ball. Points come from how many checks your setup blocks in each category, and the fill color shows your range:</p>
		<div class="template_wrap row keep-width">
			<div>
				<div class="lt_cwrap lt_template" style="--liquid-color:var(--red);--liquid-percentage:10%">
					<div class="lt_circle">
						<div class="lt_wave"></div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="lt_cwrap lt_template" style="--liquid-color:var(--orange);--liquid-percentage:0%">
					<div class="lt_circle">
						<div class="lt_wave"></div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="lt_cwrap lt_template" style="--liquid-color:var(--green);--liquid-percentage:-30%">
					<div class="lt_circle">
						<div class="lt_wave"></div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="row keep-width">
			<div>
				<div class="lt_value_2 _bg-red">0&ndash;30%: your ad blocker may not be enabled or configured</div>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="lt_value_2 _bg-orange">31&ndash;60%: okay, but you can do better</div>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="lt_value_2 _bg-green">61%+: great, you're ready to surf</div>
			</div>
		</div>
		<p>A browser extension should reach 90% or more. A DNS filter on its own tops out near 60%, because it cannot pass the cosmetic and script checks.</p>
	</div>
</details>
<details>
	<summary>How do I know if I have an ad blocker, and which one?</summary>
	<div>
		<p>Run the test. A score above 60% means something on your device is blocking ads &mdash; a browser extension, a browser with blocking built in, a VPN, or a DNS filter. To find out which one:</p>
		<ul class="keep-ls">
			<li><b>Chrome, Brave, Edge, Opera</b>: open <code>chrome://extensions</code> (or <code>edge://extensions</code>) and look for uBlock Origin, AdGuard, AdBlock or Ghostery.</li>
			<li><b>Firefox</b>: open <code>about:addons</code> and check Extensions.</li>
			<li><b>Safari</b>: Settings &rarr; Extensions, on macOS and iOS both.</li>
			<li><b>Brave and Opera</b> also block ads with no extension installed, from Shields and the built-in blocker.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>A DNS filter or a VPN-level blocker appears in none of those lists. If the test shows hosts blocked but the cosmetic and script checks failing, the blocking is happening at the network level &mdash; check your router, your Pi-hole, or your VPN app settings.</p>
	</div>
</details>
<details>
	<summary>Are you looking for a page full of ads to test your blocker?</summary>
	<div>
		<p>This is that page, with one difference: it never loads a real ad. The test uses ad-shaped decoy elements, two decoy scripts, and requests to real ad and tracker hostnames that never serve anything. You get a measurable result without funding an ad network or seeing a single advert.</p>
		<p>To confirm your blocker on a genuinely ad-heavy site, open any large news site with your blocker off and then on. This page tells you whether blocking <em>works</em>; a real site tells you how broad its filter coverage is.</p>
	</div>
</details>
<details>
	<summary>Which ad blockers and DNS filters do you recommend?</summary>
	<div>
		<p>I use <a href="https://www.dnsfilter.com/">DNSFilter</a> as my main DNS resolver, with <a href="https://nextdns.io/">NextDNS</a> as a great alternative for home users. Pi-hole and AdGuard Home are the self-hosted equivalents if you would rather run it yourself. On top of that I run these combos per platform:</p>
		<ul class="keep-ls">
			<li>Windows: Brave + uBlock Origin</li>
			<li>macOS: Brave + uBlock Origin</li>
			<li>Android: Kiwi Browser + uBlock Origin</li>
			<li>iOS: Brave with built-in ad blocking, or Safari + AdGuard extension</li>
		</ul>
		<div class="_cnt-grid-wrap">
			<a href="https://ublockorigin.com/" class="btn">uBlock Origin</a>
			<a href="https://adguard.com/en/adguard-ios/overview.html" class="btn">AdGuard for iOS</a>
		</div>
		<p>Filter lists I recommend:</p>
		<ul class="keep-ls">
			<li><a href="https://github.com/hagezi/dns-blocklists">HaGeZi Ultimate blocklists</a></li>
			<li>uBlock filters (built in)</li>
			<li><a href="https://adguard.com/kb/en/general/ad-filtering/adguard-filters/">AdGuard filters</a></li>
		</ul>
	</div>
</details>
